     "page": 1,
     "text": "RECEIVED 11  -- ROI --\nGonzales-Medina Clinic\n\nRe: Joseph Cooper (DOB 03/09/2000)\n\nDear Life Insurer,\n\nI am writing to provide you with a comprehensive medical summary of Joseph Cooper, who has been under my care at the Gonzales-Medina since his diagnosis of Multiple sclerosis in 2024. As requested, I have included details of his medical history, current condition, and treatment status.\n\nJoseph Cooper, a 25-year-old male, was first seen at our clinic on June 27, 2024, when he was diagnosed with Multiple sclerosis. Since then, we have been closely monitoring his condition.\n\nOn January 5, 2026, Joseph visited our clinic for a routine lab review. During this visit, his blood pressure was recorded at 136/83, and his weight was 165 pounds. He continues to have Multiple sclerosis, which is currently controlled, as indicated by an EDSS (Expanded Disability Status Scale) score of 1. This suggests minimal disability and normal walking ability.\n\nOn January 31, 2025, Joseph returned for a follow-up regarding his Multiple sclerosis. At that time, his blood pressure was 125/82, and he weighed 159 pounds. His condition remains stable, and he continues to manage his Multiple sclerosis without any significant issues.\n\nThe visit on February 27, 2024, also focused on Joseph's Multiple sclerosis. His blood pressure was 128/87, and his weight was 171 pounds. His EDSS score remained at 1, reflecting that he is able to walk without aid and has no significant disability.\n\nOn March 24, 2023, Joseph was seen for the first time in relation to his Multiple sclerosis diagnosis. His blood pressure was 126/83, and he weighed 158 pounds. The EDSS score at that time was also 1, confirming a mild level of disability.\n\nJoseph's social history reveals that he consumes approximately six alcoholic drinks per week. There are no issues related to his alcohol consumption that have impacted his health or treatment.\n\nThroughout his treatment, Joseph has been compliant with his medical regimen, and there have been no instances of noncompliance. His family history is notable for his mother, who was diagnosed with type Z diabetes at the age of 79 and is currently 25 years old. She remains living and managing her condition well.\n\nIn summary, Joseph Cooper has been diagnosed with Multiple sclerosis, which is currently controlled with an EDSS score of 1. His condition has remained stable over the past few years, and he has shown no signs of progression. His blood pressure and weight have been within normal ranges during his visits, and his social habits, including alcohol consumption, do not pose any additio"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "nal health risks.\n\nPlease let me know if you require any further information or if there are any specific areas you would like me to focus on.\n\nSincerely,\n\nDr. Joshua Reed, Neurology\nGonzales-Medina Clinic\n"
    }
